{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And he said, Tomorrow.</b> It is well known that the way of every person is to seek that his affliction cease immediately. They explained in the name of the Gaon Rav Samuel ben Hofni that Pharaoh thought perhaps the arrangement of the heavens brought the frogs upon Egypt, and that Moses knew that the time of their removal had arrived, and therefore he said to him, \"Glory over me\" — thinking that he [Moses] would say to him now to cut them off immediately, and therefore he [Pharaoh] prolonged it to tomorrow. But the correct interpretation in my eyes is that because he [Moses] had said, \"For when shall I entreat for you,\" Pharaoh thought that he was asking for a time, and therefore he gave him a short time — <b>and he said, Tomorrow</b>. And Moses answered him, According to your word so shall it be, for since you did not ask that they depart immediately, they shall not depart until tomorrow."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:16:43.180312803Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}